  ],
  "mathematical_audit": [
    {
      "region": "reverse_fold_cancel",
      "finding": "For arbitrary C and f_i with f_i(C(f_i x))=C x, foldr(f,C(foldl(f,x)))=C x follows by peeling f_0 from the left fold and right fold, applying the induction hypothesis to the tail at f_0(x), then applying the f_0 relation. No involutivity or invertibility of C or f_i is silently required. n=0 is reflexivity after empty folds."
    },
    {
      "region": "fold_of_sequence",
      "finding": "The sequence supplies r0=x and r(i+1)=f_i(r_i). foldl_succ_last reduces the endpoint to the prefix fold followed by the last f. The induction applies to the castSucc prefix, and the final hstep(last) is used in the correct equality direction. The base case is hzero.symm."
    },
    {
      "region": "gaps_reversed",
      "finding": "gaps(T,t)_i=snoc(t,T)_i-cons(0,t)_i contains both first and terminal arcs. The first intermediate identity complements reversed upper endpoints; the second complements reversed lower endpoints. Subtracting gives the positive-sign equality gap(reversed t)_i=gap(t)_(rev i), not its negative. Fin.cases/lastCases cover both boundaries without assuming n>0."
    },
    {
      "region": "arbitrary tuple domain",
      "finding": "The reversed-gap identity is algebraic on all real T and all tuples, even unordered tuples and negative arc differences. post uses the same real difference as actual zAt. endpoint uses the exact terminal gap T-grid(last). The inverse assumptions quantify over every real duration, so no positivity/strict-order premise is needed for this local word theorem."
    },
    {
      "region": "endpoint_as_word padding and sequence",
      "finding": "Let f_i=Phi(gap_i) o S. Starting the n+1-factor left fold at S(z) cancels its initial extra S via S^2=id, yielding Phi(gap0) then S then Phi(gap1), ending at the final Phi(gap_n). The constructed sequence r is snoc(S(post_i),endpoint); its first entry is S(z), internal transitions use post_(i+1)=S(Phi(gap_i)(post_i)), and terminal transition is exactly endpoint. Hence the fold is proved equal to the defined endpoint; no endpoint equivalence is assumed."
    },
    {
      "region": "hcomm and common reversing map",
      "finding": "hRev(u,Rx) and R^2=id give R(Phi_u x)=Phi_-u(Rx). With C=S o R and f_i=Phi_(gap_i) o S, f_i(C(f_i x)) reduces successively to Phi_u(R(Phi_u(Sx))), then Phi_u(Phi_-u(R(Sx))), then R(Sx)=S(Rx)=Cx. The supplied hInv is exactly Phi_u o Phi_-u=id, which is the required direction. No flow group law or commutation of S and Phi is used."
    },
    {
      "region": "reversed endpoint cancellation",
      "finding": "Applying endpoint_as_word to the reversed tuple and initial R(endpoint) gives a fold in reversed gap order starting from S(R(endpoint))=C(endpoint). gaps_reversed supplies exact reverse indices in Fin(n+1); core Fin.foldl_rev converts this to foldr of the original f. Substituting original endpoint_as_word lets reverse_fold_cancel apply at S(z). The result is C(Sz)=S(R(Sz))=Rz using RS=SR and S^2=id. Thus operator order and final reversal are correct."
    },
    {
      "region": "preserving_fold",
      "finding": "Base case explicitly proves the identity map preserves an arbitrary measure. The successor step composes the already preserving prefix with the last preserving factor, matching foldl_succ_last. No finite or sigma-finite measure premise is needed for composition of MeasurePreserving maps."
    },
    {
      "region": "endpoint_volume",
      "finding": "The map equality proved from endpoint_as_word identifies endpoint with preserving_fold(f) composed S, where each f_i=Phi_gap_i composed S. Each factor preserves the same arbitrary measure mu by hPhi and hSM, and the initial padding S also preserves mu. Using hS for the endpoint-as-word equality ensures the extra S factors cancel mathematically; no Jacobian/density or supplied endpoint-volume assertion is used."
    },
    {
      "region": "n=0 and degeneracies",
      "finding": "For n=0, gaps is the one-entry tuple (T), post0=z, and endpoint=Phi_T z. The padded word is Phi_T(S(Sz))=Phi_Tz; reverse cancellation gives Phi_T(R(Phi_Tz))=Rz from the same laws. T=0 is included without needing a separate Phi0=id assumption for these generic statements. After actual96 instantiation Phi0=id gives the expected actual zero-horizon behavior. Abstract X may be empty or singleton, and there is no nonzero-normal, positive-rate or rank restriction."
    },
    {
      "region": "proof ingredient discipline",
      "finding": "hS,hR,hRS,hInv,hRev,hSM,hPhi are explicit generic-helper inputs, not authorized extra public premises. Actual96 supplies all at fixed y,r, with mu equal to canonical phase product volume. The final producer must discharge them internally and use the actualEndpoint97 bridge to Gamma(T). This audit does not certify an unperformed integration."
    }
  ],
  "operator_order_summary": {
    "forward": "endpoint = Phi_d_n o S o Phi_d_(n-1) o ... o S o Phi_d_0",
    "padded_word": "foldl over f_i=Phi_d_i o S, initial S(z)",
    "common_reversal": "C=S o R",
    "factor_identity": "f_i o C o f_i = C",
    "result": "endpoint(reversed t, R(endpoint(t,z))) = R(z)",
    "preserves_actual_reflection_position_dependence": "S is one globally defined phase map at fixed reference; no constant reflection matrix or frozen normal is assumed."
  },
  "integration_contract": {
    "X": "E x E",
    "Phi": "Actual scaled harmonic Phi(y,r,u)",
    "S": "Actual zero-safe bounce S(r)",
